former Fox News anchor gretchen carlson  is credited with starting a conversation  about sexual harassment and paving the  way for accusers of Hollywood mogul  Harvey Weinstein to speak out the former  Miss America became the face of  workplace harassment last year when she  sued Roger Ailes the founder and  chairman of Fox News her lawsuit claims  that Ailes fired her after she refused  his sexual advances months later Carlson  reached a 20 million dollar settlement  Fox News parent company 21st Century Fox  the company also issued her a public  apology now Ailes denied the claims  before his death in May now Carlson is  an advocate for workplace equality she  was named one of Time Magazine's 100  most influential people in the world for  2017 Carlson has a new book called be  fierce stop harassment and take your  power back she shares a playbook for  women who want to speak out gretchen  carlson worked at CBS News from 2000 to  2005 and she joins us now good morning  welcome back it's so great to be back  although you're in this bright new shiny  studio across the street from where I  used to be yeah I hate to see all three  of you let me ask you you and I have  spoken over the past year you've you  sued one of the most powerful men in  television what made you come forward  what courage is a building process it's  not a light switch that you just turn on  overnight and I think that's really  important especially with this issue  because women are labeled troublemakers  the B word and nothing good they're not  believed when they come forward so it  takes immense courage  it's an excruciating choice but for me I  realized that my career my American  dream of 27 years of hard work that it  was going to come to an end and it  wasn't my choice and I decided that I  had to speak up if I didn't who was  going to do it and I didn't want my  children to face the same indignities  that I had faced you also you also say  that many women any woman who was spoken  up and it's not just TV news you said it  crosses all all kinds of businesses  where this is an issue but you point out  in your book that any woman who has  spoken up is no longer in her chosen  career I thought that was very  interesting and surprising Disney that's  shocking and horrifying yes it's an  equal opportunity epidemic I started  hearing from thousands of women after my  broke Gale and they were waitresses they  were bankers they were accountants  lighter teachers they were flight  attendants many of whom have thanked me  over the last year because they have all  faced this pervasive issue this is a  silent epidemic in their conduct the  perpetrators still in place so they're  still in place because we have chosen as  a culture to silence the victims either  with settlements where you're gagged  from ever saying what happened to you or  in forced arbitration which is a part of  employment contracts now and here's the  key it's secret so these are the ways in  which we've chosen to resolve sexual  harassment in our country and we need to  change explain that for everyone who  doesn't know these arbitration which all  of us sign contracts like that in our  profession as well which means what so  pervasive and employment contracts now a  clause that means that you're giving up  your Seventh Amendment right when you  sign it to go to an open jury process  listen when we start new jobs none of us  expect to get into disputes I know I  didn't right but if you do it's going to  be resolved through arbitration but  here's the key it's secret you can never  tell anyone what you're doing no one  will ever know about it 20% of the time  only the employee wins that much yeah  you say silence is the most powerful  weapon of the harasser  because guess what happens in Gail the  woman gets fired she never goes back to  her chosen career and the harasser is  left in the workplace to continue  harassing which is the case of your  settlements well so the case with my  settlement is that I can continue to  talk about this issue which I've been  doing so much work on it for 15 months I  just can't tell you the nitty-gritty  details but you can look at it online  when a complaint is when you spoke out  some of your Fox News colleagues like  Sean Hannity britt hume jean pierre  publicly doubted your stories and they  defended roger ailes at the time in an  interview Jeanine Pirro reportedly said  of the lawsuit this guy is doing 8  million things a day you really think  he's chasing her around I know you can't  speak about specifics but were you  prepared from the reaction from even  women that you worked with yes you were  you were yes because look at how we  react when women have the courage to  come forward we need to change this and  we are look at what's happening with the  Harvey Weinstein story you look at a  tipping point it's all that our water  good moment why because women are  putting their names and their faces to  this issue it's not just anonymity  anymore  I really believe as horrifying as those  revelations were that I'm optimistic  that the hard work I've been doing for  the last 15 months is paying off and the  gift of courage is contagious  now it passes along one woman at a time  and look where we are the hashtag me to  trending for the last two days and now  men are putting out their own hashtags  to say Jeff they want to be on board and  we need men to help us that with that  that is very important that they're a  part of this conversation there's a  great article in The Hollywood Reporter  by Christopher Navas a showrunner for  Shonda Rhimes on ABC she said this  entire culture is complicit if we make  this all about Harvey we've already lost  and I've heard many people say we're  focusing on that but please look at the  bigger picture of what is happening in  all industries do you agree with that I  agree with that because the thousands of  women who reached out to me their  stories were never told and I became a  voice for them in being able to publicly  tell my story I hope that all of those  women will find the courage and the  bravery now to also tell their stories  and that we will tell them for them to  eradicate this problem what are the  places in the workplace where they say  to tell your stories human resources but  in the book you say human resources is  sometimes not your friend you say  they're like the KGB my lawyer actually  called them that and I want to reach out  to all the human resource people who are  doing a good job and who do want to help  here we should what I advocate in the  book that there are maybe better ways to  solve this issue hire an ombudsman to  come in an independent person  who's not getting their paycheck from  possibly the harasser and also we need  to encourage more bystander training  more what bystander training what is  that people witness it do you think for  the 30 years of what was going on with  the allegations of Harvey Weinstein and  other stories people knew about it there  were enablers and the company covered it  up so we need to encourage bystanders to  also come forward but Gretchen I've  heard people say well we knew he might  have been a brood or a bully when it  came about  movies we might have known that maybe he  was a philanderer but we didn't know  that I've heard many people say we  didn't know it was that extreme and to  that you say I say that I'm sure that  other people knew that I'm sure that  they did because this is the way in  which it works and this is how we  continue to cover it up you um you  watched when when the Billy Bush tape  came out during the campaign you you  watch this with your kids why why did  you do that  well the first time I didn't watch it  with my kids I wanted to see what it was  for myself but I felt that it was  imperative that I showed my 12 and 14  year old kids that this is not how you  treat human beings and I think it was a  teaching moment for millions of parents  across America at least I hope it was  that they could show their children that  this is not how you respect women  because look it starts with our kids  young this is why I have an entire  chapter on how I believe we should  parent our kids in an equal fashion  because it's really more about our sons  how are we raising them to respect women  when they eventually get into the  workplace and that videotape I don't  care what your politics are sexual  harassment is a political and this is  why everyone should care about it when  somebody harasses you they don't say are  you a Republican or a Democrat they just  do it and this is why we should all care  about it and human decency supersedes  any political policy in my mind yes you  went forever you went through a very  rough time Gretchen was it all worth it  when you look back at it right in the  book how lonely it was sometimes you  didn't hear from people you expected to  hear from you felt alone many times as  you sit here today was it all worth it  for you to go through this it was Gail  because if there's one constant thing  about me I always take on a challenge  head-on and I never give up and I know  that I have been a voice for so many  women who never had one and now I'm  seeing that something good is coming of  this and I really hope that I can change  laws on Capitol Hill as well and get rid  of the secrecy so that we take this  issue out of the shadows and help so  many others gretchen carlson thank you  thanks for having me  miss North said really good to see you  at the  thank you proceeds from beef ears will  go to Gretchen's gift of courage fun the  book is on sale today wherever you like  to buy your books
 

